 The repository scan (`patterns.txt`, pattern 9, `[A-Za-z0-9+/=_-]{40,}`) reports an unbroken run of 40 or more of those characters. The prefix and a key name before `=` count towards the 40; a dot, a space or a quote ends the run. It reported every `key=value` line above, so a letters-only second project key in `sonar-project.properties` is seen by that scan alone. The exception for the real key (line 31 of the 34 lines of `exceptions.txt`, the `sonar.projectKey` allowlist in `.gitleaks.toml`) therefore has to stay exact. `tests/test_gitleaks_exceptions.py` plants near-misses in a temporary copy and fails if either exception is widened. To add a second key of this shape, add its exact text, never a wider pattern or a whole file, and keep that test passing. `.gitattributes` sets LF endings for the file types it lists (and `eol=lf` as the default for everything else) but CRLF for `*.ps1` files; the real line with CRLF endings is reported by gitleaks, not by the repository scan. If `sonar-project.properties` or `exceptions.txt` was saved with CRLF (or a lone carriage return), `tests/test_hygiene.py` says so and names two cures, and a test runs both on a planted file, once before and once after `git add`. Cure 1, `git checkout -- <file>`, throws away your edits and works only if you have not run `git add` or `git commit` on the file: measured with git 2.43 in a real clone with `core.autocrlf=true` (a planted tree gave the same with `false`), after `git add` or a commit each of `git checkout -- <file>`, `git checkout -f -- <file>`, `git restore <file>`, `git update-index --really-refresh` and `git add --renormalize <file>` left all 14 carriage returns in a CRLF file, and `git restore --worktree --source=HEAD <file>` and `git checkout HEAD -- <file>` did too for CRLF and for a committed lone CR (git sees the file as unchanged), but those last two do cure a lone CR that was only staged, while `git status` and `git diff` before the cure did not stop it from working. Cure 2, the Python one-liner in the message, keeps your edits and works in every state (write `python3` on macOS or Linux, `py -3` on Windows if `python` is not found; replace `<file>` with the path); if you had run `git add` or `git commit`, run it again after the cure. Deleting the file and running cure 1 is not offered: after `git add` it removes a CRLF but brings a lone carriage return back from the index (measured). `sed -i 's/\r$//'` is not offered: it is GNU-only and leaves a lone carriage return. The git and gitleaks measurements in this paragraph are from the author's own runs; no log is shipped. The cures were not run on real Windows. Why only those two files, measured on 2026-11-13 with gitleaks 8.30.1 by turning one file at a time into CRLF in a clean checkout: `sonar-project.properties` makes `gitleaks dir` report the key line (`test_hygiene_scan_public` fails); `exceptions.txt` makes the `grep -v -E -f exceptions.txt` step of `lint.yml` keep every line (that CI step would fail; the tests read the file with Python and still pass); `.gitleaks.toml`, `patterns.txt` (`git grep -f` found the same lines) and the `requirements-*.txt` and `.in` files made no difference (`pip` was not run on the lock files).
 
